Goals SWOT analysis
2026-9-3
[agile,SWOT,self-reflection]
<h3>My Strengths</h3>
<p>[\]
- I think one of my strongest points is that I can work for hours non-stop if I find something interesting or enjoyable. Regardless of how difficult a task is, I can push through and get it done.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- Furthermore, my deep passion for and curiosity about computers is a major strength.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- Lastly, I am a fast learner when I find a topic engaging.[\]
[\]
</p>[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
<h3>My Weaknesses</h3>[\]
<p>[\]
- I struggle to function optimally within a team due to a lack of strong communication skills.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- I also find it difficult to concentrate on tasks that don't interest me at all. It is very hard for me to learn and work under those conditions, it costs me three times as much effort and time to achieve the same result as someone else.[\]
[\]
</p>[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
<h3>My Opportunities</h3>[\]
<p>[\]
- Because I learn quickly and love doing so, I can always stay up-to-date with new developments in the industry.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- I can thrive in rapid-prototyping and R&D environments.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
</p>[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
<h3>My Threats</h3>[\]
<p>[\]
- My low tolerance for heavy bureaucracy and office politics means that highly traditional corporate environments are a risk to my productivity. I prefer to focus strictly on technical problem-solving rather than business-related tasks.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- Furthermore, while I have a theoretical and hands-on background in Agile/Scrum (including a certification), the constant interruptions of daily stand-ups, pair-programming, and backlog meetings threaten to drain my energy and disrupt my ability to do deep, focused work.[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
- Finally, while my ability to hyper-focus is a strength, I sometimes lose track of time. Accumulated overworking on the same subject frequently pushes me toward burnout, leaving me completely unmotivated.</p>[\]
Direction - where do you see yourself in the future
2026-9-4
[future,plan]
<p>
I have always wanted to be a scientist. I grew up admiring the cool scientists in white lab coats in movies and TV series <span class="alt-text">(especially the "mad scientist" types)</span>. After discovering my deep passion for computers, I knew I wanted to become a research-focused computer scientist.[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
My ultimate goal is to become an academic computer science researcher. I want to understand the deep, underlying mechanics of technology, like how a <code>HashSet</code> actually functions under the hood, rather than just using one to build a corporate application. I want to know exactly how a CPU works at a fundamental level, rather than just being an end-user. <span class="alt-text">(Or end-developer in this case)</span>[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
My concrete plan for the future is to successfully complete my propaedeutic year (propedeuse) at HZ University of Applied Sciences, and then transfer to the Computer Science bachelor's program at TU Delft. After graduating from TU Delft within three years, my goal is to climb the academic ladder until I earn a PhD in Computer Science.[\]
<br/><br/><br/>[\]
I plan to specialize in one or more of the following subjects: Cryptography, AI, Systems and Networking, Theory of Computation/Algorithms, or Programming Languages and Compilers. Currently, I am highly interested in specializing in Neuromorphic computing using memristors, with the intention of dedicating my research to that specific field.[\]
</p>[\]
Programming experience
2026-9-4
[d]
<p>My programming journey began with Scratch when I was nine years old. While it was a basic drag-and-drop tool designed to teach kids how to code, I loved creating things so much that I quickly wanted to learn actual programming.</p>
<br/><br/>[\]
<p>A couple of months later, I switched to C, where I learned fundamentals like conditional blocks and iterations. However, I hadn't yet grasped advanced concepts like pointer arithmetic, error handling, or function pointers. I then discovered C++, which I read online was essentially "C on steroids", but I struggled to grasp Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) at the time due to its complex and sometimes ambiguous syntax on C++.</p>[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<p>By chance, I came across C# .NET and Windows desktop application development with WinForms when I was 11. C# was the stepping stone I needed to finally understand the core concepts of OOP. I spent years building various Windows desktop applications by following tutorials. Over the years, I expanded my toolset alongside C#, picking up Python, Java, GML, JavaScript/TypeScript, HTML/CSS, and REST APIs.</p>[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<p>Three years ago, when I began my MBO Software Development program here in the Netherlands, the program focused only on Java. After gaining enough knowledge of OOP and spending a lot of time with high-level languages, I felt the urge to dive back into low-level programming, but seriously this time. I tried using C and C++ for real-life projects, but they felt a bit "old and cranky" due to their standard libraries and legacy language features. That is when I discovered Rust. It was love at first sight, and we have been in a healthy relationship ever since.</p>[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<p>I really struggle with completing what I started. Once I figure out the underlying mechanics of a problem, I often move on. Because of this, my backlog consists more of experimental learning projects and cloned programs rather than fully polished applications. However, after getting comfortable with Rust and system-level concepts, I have built a few projects worth mentioning:</p>[\]
<br/><br/>[\]
<ul class="left">[\]
  <li><strong>HEMLC:</strong> A transpiler for HEML.</li>[\]
  <li><strong>RIDBF:</strong> A highly experimental, read-only data structure and transport library built in and for TypeScript (via NPM).</li>[\]
</ul>[\]